landed strict JSON gate and REWRITES source text while stamping the capture status `exact`. Rewriting and continuing is the same class this Story has now been rejected for three times at three different members — numbers, extension values, and now text at the projection entry. Delegate the envelope and body decodes to `environ.DecodeStrictObject` (the reviewer proved it clean for this use in `logs/fixprobe-strict-decode.log`), add lone-surrogate rows for a text member and an envelope member and duplicate-member rows for the envelope and the body, each with one narrowing mutant, and fix the TRACEABILITY row that claims \"never rewritten\" so it names the gate that now actually enforces it.\n4|P2-a — the seven inherited advisory groups are mentioned nowhere in the results, matrix, TRACEABILITY or LOGBOOK. This is the third leaf on this Story to drop them silently, and this time it is worse than bookkeeping: production prose on the story_final patch now states that the final leaf did something it did not. Write one decision line per advisory group (1-6, 7b, 7c) in the results — closed with its test, or deferred with the owner and the reason — and rewrite `clonesnap/project.go:41-43` and `clonesnap/TRACEABILITY.md:132-147` so they state what the tree actually does. A sentence in shipped code that describes work you did not do is a defect, not a documentation nit.\n5|P2-b — a §7.8 registry binding discharges the 8 MiB adapter-frame clause with a test that constructs no frame, and the README publishes it as \"Section 7.8 at 2/2, bound to internal/cloneproject\". Re-bind `7.8#1` to the sessadapter frame-bound tests with a sessadapter production owner, or record it as `partial 1/2` with the gap named. Then re-derive the digest and update `traceability_test.go`, `main_test.go` and the README pins and prose to match. Remember that on a final leaf the registry, the pin, the tracecheck ratios and the README figures are the deliverable, not decoration: a binding that names a cla